# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tremove tag[2] fid[4]
size[4] Rremove tag[2]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Tremove` asks the server to remove the file <fid> refers to and to clunk <fid>, which happens even if the remove fails.
It is a clunk (see clunk(5)) with the side effect of removing the file if permissions allow.
The fid may be open or not.
The message is 9P2000's, types 122 and 123, unchanged, and needs no extension.

The framework checks only that <fid> is known, and lets the file server decide the rest.
VectraOS's servers that remove files, tmpfs(4), fsd(4) and dosfs(4) when writable, refuse to remove the root they serve and a directory that is not empty; fsd asks for write permission in the file's directory, as Plan 9's servers do.
A server that removes nothing refuses every `Tremove`, and the fid is clunked all the same.

When a file is open as other fids, on this connection or another, they keep working.
tmpfs and fsd take the file out of its directory at once but keep its contents until the last fid open on it is clunked, as POSIX's `unlink` does; fsd keeps it on the volume as an orphan for that time, and reaps those a crash leaves at the branch's next attach.
A fid not open is another matter: an open of it after the remove finds no file, though share(5)'s `Tjoin` still reaches an open file that has been removed.
Plan 9's own servers differ, failing the other fids at once with a `phase error`.

A `Tremove` of an open fid lets go of the connection's locks on the file, as any clunk of one does (see lock(5)).

# SEE ALSO

intro(5), clunk(5), walk(5), open(5), lock(5), fsd(4), tmpfs(4)

# DIAGNOSTICS

A VectraOS server answers with these:

: `unknown fid`
  <fid> is not in use.
: `permission denied`
  The root of the tree; no write permission in the directory; or a server that removes nothing.
: `file already exists`
  A directory that is not empty, which is the wording of the status VectraOS's servers have for it.
: `file does not exist`
  The file has already been removed.
